Fork continuous integration
===========================

This fork runs .github/workflows/ci.yml on its own L3 Linux runners.
CI_PROVIDER is the literal l3; all jobs use literal l3-ubuntu-26.04 labels.
The existing Runs service is responsible for providing AWS Spot instances.
Static label validation alone does not prove the backing instance is Spot.

Run `mise install`, `mise run setup-hooks`, then `mise run check` before
pushing. The normal pre-push hook runs the same lightweight check. It checks
workflow syntax, shell syntax/static analysis and own-runner routing; it does
not compile PostgreSQL or validate a produced WASM distribution. No project.ts
or fabricated build receipt is used. The actual build/test jobs are required
by the aggregate Check, including failure or cancellation of prerequisites.

Coverage and capacity
---------------------

Every push, pull request and manual dispatch runs the following work. The
minimal sanity job also runs the lightweight workflow checks before building.

* Sanity: debug Meson, automatic optional features disabled, TAP enabled;
  ninja all/testprep, setup tests, cube regression and pg_ctl start/stop TAP.
* Native autoconf: full world-bin/check-world, ASAN, assertions, injection
  points, debug, NLS, six-block segment size, NUMA/liburing and the original
  Linux optional language/security/compression libraries; copy-file-range
  backup testing.
* Native Meson: full test suite, UBSAN alignment/undefined, assertions,
  injection points, LLVM/NUMA, explicit optional libraries and io_uring initdb.
* Native 32-bit Meson: the same UBSAN test suite inside native i386 Debian
  Bookworm userspace on the x86 Linux runner, without LLVM/NUMA and with LANG=C.
  This replaces the original Trixie multiarch and special Perl 5.40 executable;
  Bookworm's native 32-bit Perl is used. This is a userspace/toolchain change.
* Compiler warnings: four GCC/Clang, assertions and DTrace combinations with
  -Werror; MinGW cross compilation; documentation, headerscheck and
  cpluspluscheck. Ubuntu's packaged MinGW targets MSVCRT, not the original
  UCRT toolchain. Cross compilation disables ICU, readline and zlib because
  their target libraries are not supplied. It does not execute Windows code.
* WASM: the existing build-pglite.sh, recursive extension submodules and the
  exact digest of its existing electricsql/pglite-builder:3.1.74-7 image.
  The shell fails on build-command errors; the libc and pgcrypto chained
  commands explicitly propagate failure. The Node smoke executes the built
  pg_config --version, verifies WASM/data output and instantiates the core
  module with its bundled PostgreSQL catalog. Distribution artifacts are kept.
  This repository has no PGlite JavaScript/browser SQL integration harness;
  the smoke does not claim SQL, browser, or every extension runtime coverage.

The native jobs retain four-CPU execution capacity and eight test workers.
The WASM job explicitly requests the 32-core L3 label and caps its container
at 32 CPUs. There are seven job executions, including the aggregate Check.
The compiler combinations run sequentially and stop on failure. Native suites
retain PG_TEST_EXTRA kerberos/ldap/ssl/libpq_encryption/load_balance/oauth.
Failures, skipped jobs and cancellations cannot produce a successful Check.

Platform gaps and validation
----------------------------

The old Cirrus definitions are preserved under cirrus-archive/ for coverage
comparison. They are inert: root .cirrus.yml, .cirrus.tasks.yml and .cirrus.star
activation files are absent. This fork does not enable or call Cirrus, GCP,
GitHub-hosted, or another provider's runner.

Own L3 Linux runners cannot supply native FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, macOS,
Windows MSVC or Windows MinGW execution. The original FreeBSD running-server
suite, debug tree-copy/expression/parallel-query instrumentation and link-mode
upgrade variants are not represented by the Linux jobs. Platform-specific
libc, filesystem, compiler, terminal and runtime behavior therefore remains
unverified. The original native platform coverage cannot be retained while
restricting execution to the currently available own Linux Spot runners.
The MinGW cross build is only partial compile coverage, not native parity.

The initial conversion has only passed lightweight syntax/static/routing
checks. No native build, container build, full test suite, produced-artifact
Node smoke, or remote runner/Spot proof has run yet. Those checks require a
scheduled remote CI slot before this draft can be described as validated.
